{
  "requirement": "The SDK presents node inputs using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\nunder {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content.format`.\nIts text and fingerprint describe the same retained observation.\n\n```rust,ignore\nlet contents = domain.node_content(&node_id)?;\nlet text = contents.format();\n```\n\nLoading and fallible preparation occur in `Domain::node_content` under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.node_content`. For inputs queued on an ask\nbuilder, these failures return `ValidationError::Graph`. During a `read_node`\ntool call, they are returned to the agent as a tool error, so it can continue\nand recover.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "Add `{impl}` to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[Ask<'a, 'd, D, R, P>]#2/fn[execute]` — Clause: \"The SDK presents node inputs using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\" (`let contents = domain.node_content(&node_id)?; let text = contents.format();`), with failures of loading returned as `ValidationError::Graph` for inputs queued on an ask builder. No implementation edge exists. An alternative that keeps the observable behavior for simple domains but violates the clause: render queued inputs with `Debug`, or re-read the node via a second `node_content` call, or build text from a domain-specific accessor, instead of calling `format()` on the value returned by the same read that produced the fingerprint. `Ask::execute` is the root that performs this delegation. It calls `prepare_read` (mapping errors to `ValidationError::Graph`), calls `snapshot.content.format()`, and pairs the text with `snapshot.fingerprint` in `PreparedInput`. No other root covers the ask-builder path.\n\nAdd `{impl}` to `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/tools.rs#impl[SessionTools<'a, D>]/fn[read_node]` — Clause: \"During a `read_node` tool call, they are returned to the agent as a tool error\" and node inputs are presented \"using the returned value's `NodeContents::format`\". An alternative that gives the same results for simple domains but violates the clause: have the tool serialize node data, or fetch the text through a separate path such as direct source resolution, instead of `format()` on the value returned by `prepare_read`. Another violation: let a load failure abort the session instead of returning a tool error. `SessionTools::read_node` is a separate caller from `Ask::execute`. It prepares the read once and formats `snapshot.content.format()`. It records `Dependency::Node` with the snapshot's fingerprint. It returns failures as `io::Error`, which the tool layer reports as a tool error. The other proposed root does not cover this path.",
  "references": "440d09ce653c637a5d9620610fcea9dc80d85be02cf4f951f4c946639df7dfc3",
